# TUI-DESIGN-3 §4 (D-K) idle: `/s` (an alias) → the /status block; typing `/s` shows the ghost arrow ` → /status`; `/p d` opens the
# decisions tab (App-local, alias- and case-aware, F3), a second `/p d` collapses it; `/budgett` (a fixable error) keeps the draft — Ctrl-C
# clears it; `/exit` leaves 0
expect \x1b\[\?25l
expect Say hi
expect sess \$
sleep 0.3
# `/` and `s` are two keystrokes: `composer:palette` (keys/resolve.ts) opens the palette on the `/` key alone, and the
# ghost arrow is drawn only while the palette is open (App.tsx). MINIMAL, MARKED (S4, TUI-DESIGN-4 §4.7 E9): one
# `send /s` write is a single input event that used to open nothing; it now opens the palette with `s` as the query,
# so the two-keystroke form below is kept deliberately — it is the sequence this scenario was written for.
send /
expect Tab picks
send s
expect → /status
sleep 0.3
send \r
expect \[ui\](?:\x1b\[[0-9;]*m)* status
sleep 0.3
send /p d
sleep 0.2
send \r
expect ▾(?:\x1b\[[0-9;]*m)* decisions
sleep 0.3
send /p d
sleep 0.2
send \r
expect ▸(?:\x1b\[[0-9;]*m)* jev|─{10}
sleep 0.3
send /budgett
sleep 0.2
# TUI-DESIGN-4 §4.2 (D-X) is landed: `/budgett` matches nothing, which is S-NONE, and S-NONE's Enter is the §12
# TOAST — it never submits, so the `[ui] error:` item does not appear while the card is open. That is the whole
# point of §4.1's safety theorem. Esc closes the card; the same Enter then takes round 3's submit path and the
# error item arrives, with the draft kept.
send \r
expect nothing to pick
sleep 0.3
send \x1b
sleep 0.2
send \r
expect unknown command|no such command|error
expect (?:›|>) (?:\x1b\[[0-9;]*m)*/budgett
# the palette is closed, so this Ctrl-C is the ordinary CLEAR_DRAFT rule and is unchanged by §4.7 E12 (which only
# fires with the card OPEN and the whole draft one token)
send \x03
expect Follow-up, question|Say hi
send /exit
sleep 0.2
send \r
eof
